<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Grimnitz</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1529">1529</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Grimnitz was concluded on 26 August 1529 at the Grimnitz hunting lodge between Elector Joachim I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> and the dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a>. Brandenburg gave up its claim to feudal overlordship over Pomerania, and in return the Griffin dukes recognised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">Hohenzollerns</a>' right to inherit the duchy if their own line died out. Brandenburg pressed this claim after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-griffins">Griffins</a> became extinct in 1637.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-grimnitz.jpg" alt="Treaty of Grimnitz" width="320" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lucas Cranach the Elder · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lucas_Cranach_(I)_-_Joachim_I_Nestor_-_Jagdschloss_Grunewald.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a> and the Hohenzollern electors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> had long disputed whether the Pomeranian dukes held their duchy as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> of Brandenburg or directly from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a>. The Treaties of Prenzlau of 1472 and 1479 had ruled for Brandenburg, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-pyritz">Treaty of Pyritz</a> of 1493 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-cicero">John Cicero</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogislaw-x">Bogislaw X</a> released the dukes from that obligation while giving the Hohenzollern the succession should the Pomeranian line die out.</p><p>When children were born to Bogislaw, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-cicero">John Cicero</a> worked to keep him from gaining imperial immediacy, and Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> invested both houses with the duchy and collected the tax from both. Bogislaw's sons Barnim IX and George I carried on the dispute with Joachim I Nestor after 1523, until the dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a>, Eric I and Henry IV, mediated a settlement at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a>' hunting seat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uckermark">Uckermark</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>Besides the exchange of immediacy for the right of succession, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> was to be told before any investiture of Pomeranian dukes, could touch the flags they received from the emperor, could send an envoy when the Pomeranian nobility swore allegiance, and was itself to be invested with the expectancy by the emperor. The treaty was to be announced and renewed at every new investiture, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> could bear the Pomeranian title and arms wherever the dukes were not present.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>At the Diet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> in 1530 Barnim and George received their duchy as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> for the first time. Joachim protested, seconded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-the-pious">George, Margrave of Brandenburg-Ansbach</a>, and as the dukes took the flags of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szczecin">Stettin</a>, Kashubia, Rügen and other lands from the emperor he laid his hands on each; the ceremony was repeated at every investiture.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogislaw-xiv">Bogislaw XIV</a> died in 1637, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-william-elector-of-brandenburg">George William, Elector of Brandenburg</a>, claimed the duchy under the treaty. Bogislaw had allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> in the Treaty of Stettin of 1630 and named Sweden to govern after his death, and Sweden refused to give it up. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> in 1648 divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a> between Sweden and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, a partition carried out by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-stettin-1653">Treaty of Stettin</a> in 1653.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Grimnitz” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By the Treaty of Pyritz in 1493 Brandenburg gave up its claim to overlordship in return for a right of succession should the Griffins die out, confirmed by the <b>Treaty of Grimnitz</b> in 1529.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-pomerania">Duchy of Pomerania · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Treaty of Grimnitz</b> in 1529 freed the dukes from Brandenburg's feudal claims in exchange for a promise of succession, and Pomerania adopted Lutheranism in 1534.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-pomerania-stettin">Duchy of Pomerania-Stettin</a></li></ul></section>
